<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    updatePeriodMillis is 0 on purpose. The platform floor is half an hour, and
    a card that changes on its own while you are looking at it is the opposite
    of what a card is for. It redraws when tapped, and not otherwise.

    minHeight is what the longest card needs, not what looks small. The card
    text autosizes down to 12sp, and at 12sp the 127-character card wraps to
    seven lines of 17.4dp at 180dp wide: 122dp of text, 28dp of padding, and
    the 48dp tap box of the deck name beneath it.

    minResizeHeight is a different question, and declaring only the first
    answered it wrongly: a provider with no minResize is resizable no smaller
    than its own minimum, so sizing the default for the longest card made that
    the smallest the widget could ever be. On the launcher grid it came out at
    246x293dp for a median card that fills under a third of it. The floor is
    now the room two lines need, 111dp by the same arithmetic, and CardWidget
    sets the card's maxLines from the height the host reports, so a card too
    long for a small widget ends in an ellipsis rather than being cut with
    nothing to show for it. The width keeps its floor: a monospace card much
    narrower than 180dp wraps into noise whatever its height.
-->
<appwidget-provider xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:initialLayout="@layout/card_widget"
    android:minWidth="180dp"
    android:minHeight="200dp"
    android:minResizeHeight="111dp"
    android:resizeMode="horizontal|vertical"
    android:updatePeriodMillis="0"
    android:widgetCategory="home_screen"
    android:description="@string/widget_description" />
